# AWS Zero to Hero — Day 0: Complete Course Roadmap

## 1. Course Overview

This is a **30-day AWS course** focused entirely on the **DevOps engineering perspective**. The goal is not certification prep — it is **job readiness**. Each day covers:

- Core theory and concepts
- Hands-on practicals and live projects
- Real-world DevOps use cases
- Interview questions and answers

The course covers the AWS services that DevOps engineers interact with most frequently on the job — compute, networking, security, CI/CD pipelines, monitoring, containers, and infrastructure as code.

---

## 2. Full 30-Day Curriculum

---

### Week 1 — Foundations (Days 1–7)

#### Day 1 — Introduction to AWS & Cloud Computing
- What is private cloud vs public cloud
- Differences between private and public cloud
- Why organizations move to the cloud (and when they should)
- Creating an AWS account
- Navigating the AWS Management Console

#### Day 2 — IAM (Identity and Access Management)
- What is IAM and why it is critical
- Creating users and groups on AWS
- Writing and attaching IAM policies
- Restricting access with least-privilege principles
- Zero-access / zero-privilege best practices

#### Day 3 — EC2 (Elastic Compute Cloud)
- What is an EC2 instance and why it is widely used
- Different types of EC2 instances
- Launching and configuring EC2 instances
- **Project:** Deploy a web application (e.g., Jenkins) on EC2 and expose it to the internet

#### Day 4 — AWS Networking & VPC (Virtual Private Cloud)
- VPC concepts and architecture
- Public and private subnets
- Route tables and internet gateways
- Networking infrastructure design for applications

#### Day 5 — AWS Security
- Network ACLs (Access Control Lists)
- Security Groups
- IAM policies for access restriction
- Preventing unauthorized external access to applications
- Core principle: security concepts transfer across clouds and on-premises environments

#### Day 6 — AWS Route 53 (DNS)
- What Route 53 is and how DNS works on AWS
- Registering and managing domains
- Creating DNS records
- Advanced features: health checks and routing policies

#### Day 7 — Project: Full Stack Deployment on AWS
- **Integrates:** IAM + EC2 + VPC + Security Groups + Route 53
- Deploy a real application as a DevOps engineer would on the job
- Configure networking, security, DNS, and compute end-to-end
- Goal: mirror a real-world production environment setup

---

### Week 2 — Storage, IaC & CI/CD (Days 8–14)

#### Day 8 — Amazon S3 (Simple Storage Service)
- What S3 is and its wide range of use cases beyond simple file storage
- Storage classes and lifecycle policies
- **Project:** Host a static website on S3 with CDN integration

#### Day 9 — AWS CloudFormation (Infrastructure as Code)
- What infrastructure as code (IaC) means
- Writing CloudFormation templates
- Automating the provisioning of all resources covered so far (IAM, EC2, VPC, Route 53)
- **Project:** Deploy infrastructure using CloudFormation templates

#### Day 10 — AWS CLI (Command Line Interface)
- Introduction to the AWS CLI
- Common CLI commands for managing AWS resources
- How CLI relates to and complements CloudFormation

#### Day 10/11 — AWS Elastic Beanstalk
- What Elastic Beanstalk is (fully managed deployment and scaling service)
- When and why to use it
- **Project:** Deploy an application using Elastic Beanstalk

#### Day 11 — AWS CodeCommit
- What CodeCommit is (AWS-managed Git repository)
- Setting up and using CodeCommit for source control

#### Day 12 — AWS CodePipeline
- Building an automated CI/CD pipeline on AWS
- **Project:** Create a CodePipeline that triggers on code changes

#### Day 13 — AWS CodeBuild
- What CodeBuild is (managed build service)
- Configuring build environments and build specifications
- **Project:** Build and test code using CodeBuild

#### Day 14 — AWS CodeDeploy
- What CodeDeploy is and deployment strategies it supports
- Deploying applications to EC2 instances
- **Project:** End-to-end CI/CD pipeline using CodeCommit + CodePipeline + CodeBuild + CodeDeploy

---

### Week 3 — Monitoring, Serverless & Databases (Days 15–21)

#### Day 15 — AWS CloudWatch (Monitoring)
- What CloudWatch is and why monitoring matters
- Collecting metrics from AWS resources
- Creating alarms and notification triggers
- CloudWatch dashboards

#### Day 16 — AWS Lambda (Serverless Computing)
- What serverless computing means
- Writing Lambda functions in Python
- Triggering Lambda functions based on events
- Use cases for DevOps engineers

#### Day 17 — AWS CloudWatch Events & EventBridge
- Event-driven architecture concepts
- Combining CloudWatch and Lambda for automated workflows
- **Project:** Serverless event-driven pipeline using EventBridge
- This is a high-value interview project — real-world companies use this pattern extensively

#### Day 18 — AWS CloudTrail & AWS Config
- What CloudTrail is (API activity logging)
- Auditing and tracking changes to AWS resources
- AWS Config rules for enforcing compliance policies

#### Day 19 — AWS DynamoDB
- What DynamoDB is (fully managed NoSQL database)
- DevOps-specific use case: **Terraform state locking** with DynamoDB
- Basic CRUD operations on DynamoDB tables
- Scope kept to DevOps engineering relevance

#### Day 20 — AWS ECS (Elastic Container Service)
- Overview of ECS for container orchestration
- Brief project to understand the basics

#### Day 21 — AWS ECR + EKS (Elastic Kubernetes Service)
- What ECR (Elastic Container Registry) is
- Why EKS is preferred over ECS for most real-world DevOps roles
- Deploying containerized applications to EKS
- **Note:** Watch the containers/Kubernetes fundamentals series before this day

---

### Week 4 — Scaling, Migration & Best Practices (Days 22–30)

#### Day 23 — AWS Systems Manager
- Managing secrets, credentials, and API keys
- Parameter Store and Secrets Manager use cases
- How DevOps engineers use Systems Manager in production
- **Project:** Integrate Systems Manager into a DevOps workflow

#### Day 25 — AWS Auto Scaling
- What Auto Scaling is and when to use it
- Types of scaling supported by AWS:
- Manual scaling
- Scheduled scaling
- Dynamic / reactive scaling
- Predictive scaling
- Real-world use case: scale up for peak traffic (e.g., holiday seasons), scale down when load drops
- Which scaling type fits which scenario

#### Day 26 — AWS RDS (Relational Database Service)
- Overview of RDS and managed database options
- DevOps-scoped coverage: provisioning, backups, and integration with applications

#### Day 27 — AWS Elastic Load Balancer (ELB)
- History and evolution of load balancers
- Types of load balancers on AWS:

| Load Balancer | Layer | Best For |
|---|---|---|
| Classic Load Balancer | L4/L7 | Legacy workloads |
| Network Load Balancer (NLB) | L4 | High-performance, low-latency traffic |
| Application Load Balancer (ALB) | L7 | HTTP/HTTPS routing, microservices |

- High availability, fault tolerance, and scalability concepts
- When to choose ALB vs NLB

#### Day 29 — AWS Cloud Migration
- Cloud migration strategies (the 6 R's: Rehost, Replatform, Refactor, Repurchase, Retire, Retain)
- Migration tools available in the AWS ecosystem
- **Interview focus:** How to explain a cloud migration strategy confidently
- After this session, the goal is to answer "Walk me through a cloud migration you've done or would approach" convincingly in an interview

#### Day 30 — AWS Best Practices & Career Prep
- AWS best practices for DevOps engineers
- How to continue building projects on AWS after the course
- Resources for deepening AWS knowledge
- Interview preparation guidance for AWS DevOps roles

---

## 3. Learning Approach

### Focus: DevOps Engineering, Not Certifications
The course deliberately excludes AWS services primarily used for ML, AI Ops, and data engineering. Every topic covered is something a DevOps engineer encounters regularly on the job.

### Each Day Includes
- **Theory** — concept explained from first principles
- **Practical demo** — hands-on in the AWS console or CLI
- **Project** — a realistic deliverable that can be referenced in interviews
- **Interview questions** — where relevant, specific Q&A to prepare for hiring rounds

### Cross-Cloud Portability
Networking and security concepts are taught in a way that transfers across AWS, Azure, GCP, and on-premises environments. Learning the fundamentals once means adapting to any cloud is much faster.
